hi this is pressure Locker let's discuss  the staircase paradox imagine a  staircase composed of two steps that  have a horizontal distance of four and a  vertical height of three what's the  length of the staircase we can solve  this problem by breaking it down into  its horizontal distance of four and its  vertical distance of three clearly the  total length of the staircase is the sum  which is 7 now let's redraw the  staircase instead of having two steps  we'll draw it as having four steps  what's the length of the staircase now  once again we can look at the horizontal  distance of the steps and the vertical  distance of the steps and see that the  length of the staircase is still 7 in  fact even if we change the staircase to  have more steps it will always be true  that the total length of the staircase  is 7 even if it's atomically small steps  now comes the interesting point in  geometry class if you had a right  triangle with a horizontal leg 2 4 and a  vertical leg of 3 you would solve for  the diagonal by the Pythagorean theorem  and you would find that the length of  that diagonal is 5 and this is a paradox  because how can the lengths be 5 and  also 7 at the same time if you liked  this video you should check out my book  40 paradoxes and logic probability and  game theory please subscribe to my  channel I make videos on math and game  theory you can support me at patreon to  get exclusive rewards catch me on my  blog bind your decisions on Twitter at  pressure salt Locker and you can get all  of my books at Amazon
 

